Chapter: GopSourcePreviewProbe
  cites Works chapter GopFiles
  cites Works chapter GopDraw
  cites Works chapter SyntaxHighlight
  cites Kernel chapter BitmapFont
  cites Foreword chapter CCE

 The smallest program that puts highlighted Codex source on a real
 framebuffer through the real drawing path.

 The file manager's source preview is otherwise reachable only behind a
 first-boot wizard that wants a typed passphrase and an Ed25519 keypair,
 which is a great deal of machine to drive in order to look at one
 screen. This calls `gfl-source-lines` directly, with a buffer of ASCII
 source bytes exactly as a sector read would hand it over, so every line
 between the bytes and the pixels is the shipping one: the classifier,
 the run walk, `syn-colour`, and `gop-draw-text`.

 The bytes are built the way the medium holds them -- one ASCII byte per
 character, through `to-unicode` -- because that is what the image writer
 puts there and what the preview must read back. Filling the buffer with
 CCE code points instead would make the probe agree with a reader that
 was wrong.

 We say:

Section: The Source Under Glass

 Five lines, chosen so the frame cannot pass by accident: a header, a
 prose line whose first word is `cites`, the same word as a real
 directive one space further in, a keyword-and-number line, and a string.
 If the classifier were bypassed and the whole buffer drawn in one
 colour, the prose and directive lines would be indistinguishable, which
 is precisely what the previous hex view could not tell you either.

  probe-src : Text
  probe-src = "Chapter: Demo\n cites is an ordinary word\n  cites Foreword chapter CCE\n  let x = 42\n  msg = \"hi\" & show x\n"

  probe-fill : Integer, Text, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  probe-fill (buf) (t) (i) (len) (o) =
    if i >= len then o
    else let u = to-unicode (char-code (char-at t i))
    in let b = if u >= 256 then 63 else u
    in let p = poke-byte buf o b
    in probe-fill buf t (i + 1) len (o + 1)

Section: Entry

  probe-fb : Integer
  probe-fb = 3204448256

  opening : [Device.Port, Device.Mmio] Integer
  opening =
    let buf = alloc-bytes 1024
    in let n = probe-fill buf probe-src 0 (text-length probe-src) 0
    in let font = cbf-init
    in let bg = gop-fill-rect probe-fb 640 0 0 640 480 2107448
    in let d = gfl-source-lines probe-fb 640 480 640 1 font buf 0 n 0
    in probe-spin 0

 The probe has to still be running when the frame is taken, because
 codex-vm captures the framebuffer at the delay and a guest that has
 already exited leaves nothing to capture. A short spin ran out in under
 a second and produced no screenshot at all, which reads exactly like a
 drawing path that wrote nothing.

  probe-spin : Integer -> Integer
  probe-spin (i) = if i >= 0 then probe-spin (i + 1) else 0
